\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We analyze how attention affects cross-architecture performance variance using models from the hybrid recurrent model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. Our analysis spans three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2), five attention ratios (0\% pure recurrent, 4\%, 7.7\%, 14.3\%, 25\%), and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters). In the h$k$-1 notation used throughout, one attention layer is interleaved per every $k{+}1$ layers; h3-1 denotes 25\% attention. All models within each scale share identical training data and hyperparameters; they differ only in architecture and attention configuration. We evaluate on 10 benchmarks spanning retrieval, comprehension, reasoning, and mixed task categories, plus one supplementary benchmark (COPA, $n = 100$).

\subsection{Task-Specific Convergence}\label{ssec:task_convergence}

\subsection{Per-Example Divergence}\label{ssec:per_example}

Do architectures converge by improving on the same examples? We compare each architecture's pure-recurrent baseline against its h3-1 hybrid on 5{,}153 LAMBADA examples, classifying each as helped (correct only in hybrid), hurt (correct only in pure), or unchanged.

Each architecture displays a distinct profile of per-example changes (Table~\ref{tab:per_example}). GDN loses a net 153 examples (462 helped, 615 hurt), RetNet loses 69 (426/495), and HGRN2 gains 153 (548/395). HGRN2 is the only architecture for which attention produces a net per-example benefit. Pairwise Jaccard similarity is low: mean $\bar{J} \leq 0.104$ for helped, $\leq 0.096$ for hurt, indicating ${\sim}90$\% non-overlap. Overlap significantly exceeds the independence baseline ($J_{\text{null}} \approx 0.05$, all $z > 5.9$, $p < .0001$; Appendix~\ref{app:jaccard_permutation}), but normalizing by the theoretical upper bound $J_{\max} = \min(|A|,|B|)/\max(|A|,|B|)$, observed overlap reaches only 5--7\% of the achievable range above chance (normalized ES 0.065 helped, 0.062 hurt; per-pair details in Appendix~\ref{app:normalized_es}), confirming that low Jaccard cannot be attributed to set-size constraints.

No surface-level feature (token count, word frequency, syntactic complexity) reliably separates helped from hurt examples. However, pure-model confidence on the target token is strongly predictive within each architecture: hurt examples have lower target-token probability among pure-correct examples ($d = 0.83$--$0.92$), and helped examples have higher probability among pure-wrong examples ($d = 0.73$--$0.77$); see Appendix~\ref{app:confidence_margin}. A Kruskal-Wallis test confirms cross-architecture structure in both hurt ($p = .0005$) and helped ($p = .020$) distributions. The confidence margin thus identifies a per-example predictor of which examples will be affected: attention disrupts marginally correct predictions and rescues near-miss errors, but with architecture-specific thresholds that produce the observed cross-architecture divergence. This structured per-example disagreement despite similar aggregate performance constitutes predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive} arising from architectural variation rather than random initialization \citep{damour2022underspecification}.

\paragraph{Multiplicity spectrum.} Extending the per-example analysis to BoolQ ($N = 3{,}270$) and HellaSwag ($N = 10{,}042$) reveals that predictive multiplicity is not binary but varies systematically across tasks (Table~\ref{tab:multiplicity}). The ambiguity $\alpha$ \citep{marx2020predictive} spans an order of magnitude: from 0.10 (HellaSwag) through 0.21 (ARC-Easy) and 0.30 (LAMBADA) to 0.49 (BoolQ). Yet pairwise Jaccard remains uniformly low ($\bar{J} \leq 0.10$, all $p < .01$), indicating that architectures affect different examples regardless of how many examples are affected. HellaSwag's near-zero $\alpha$ rules out measurement noise as the source of multiplicity on the other benchmarks. Within each benchmark, $\alpha$ is stable across attention ratios (range $\leq 0.04$), confirming that multiplicity magnitude depends on task, not attention dose \citep[cf.][]{gorbett2026crossmodel}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Predictive multiplicity at 1.3B (h3-1). $\alpha$: ambiguity \citep{marx2020predictive}. $\bar{J}$: mean pairwise Jaccard of helped~(h) and hurt~(u) sets. Benchmarks ordered by $\alpha$.}
\label{tab:multiplicity}

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We analyze how attention affects cross-architecture performance variance using models from the hybrid recurrent model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. Our analysis spans three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2), five attention ratios (0\% pure recurrent, 4\%, 7.7\%, 14.3\%, 25\%), and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters). In the h$k$-1 notation used throughout, one attention layer is interleaved per every $k{+}1$ layers; h3-1 denotes 25\% attention. All models within each scale share identical training data and hyperparameters; they differ only in architecture and attention configuration. We evaluate on 10 benchmarks spanning retrieval, comprehension, reasoning, and mixed task categories, plus one supplementary benchmark (COPA, $n = 100$).

\subsection{Task-Specific Convergence}\label{ssec:task_convergence}

We first ask whether adding attention layers reduces performance differences between architectures, and whether any such reduction is uniform across tasks. For each benchmark, we compute the cross-architecture standard deviation ($\sigma$) at each attention ratio and measure the Spearman rank correlation ($\rho$) between attention ratio and $\sigma$ across the five configurations.

Table~\ref{tab:convergence} reports results for all benchmarks at 1.3B scale. LAMBADA is the only benchmark exhibiting monotonic convergence: $\rho = -1.0$ (exact permutation test, $p = .017$, two-tailed, $n = 5$, 120 permutations). Cross-architecture $\sigma$ drops from 0.024 to 0.005, a 79\% reduction. The 95\% BCa percentile bootstrap confidence interval for $\rho$ is $[-1.0, -0.7]$ (10{,}000 resamples), which excludes zero. After Bonferroni correction for six confirmatory benchmarks, the adjusted $p = .100$ does not survive strict Bonferroni correction at $\alpha = 0.05$. Benjamini--Hochberg false discovery rate control yields a similar adjusted $p$ (0.102), as only one of six benchmarks approaches nominal significance. The confidence interval provides a complementary characterization of the convergence effect, indicating a reliably negative association even though the corrected $p$-value does not reach conventional significance.

LAMBADA is a pure context-retrieval task \citep{paperno2016lambada}; attention provides direct token-level access to the full context, consistent with convergence reflecting compensation for differences in how recurrent architectures store contextual information. No other benchmark shows significant convergence (all $|\rho| \leq 0.7$, $p \geq .233$). ARC-Easy and MMLU exhibit moderate negative correlations ($\rho = -0.7$) but non-monotonic trajectories; COPA shows a similar pattern ($\rho = -0.67$) but is limited by $n = 100$ (Figure~\ref{fig:task_specificity}). The decoupling analysis (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}) requires a converging benchmark and is demonstrated on LAMBADA; per-example divergence itself extends to ARC-Easy (\S\ref{ssec:per_example}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_task_specificity.pdf}
\caption{Spearman $\rho$ between attention ratio and cross-architecture $\sigma$ for each benchmark at 1.3B scale. LAMBADA ($\rho = -1.0$; BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0, -0.7]$) is the only benchmark with a negative correlation whose confidence interval excludes zero. Benchmarks are grouped by task category.}
\label{fig:task_specificity}
\end{figure}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-architecture convergence across benchmarks (1.3B scale). $\rho$: Spearman correlation between attention ratio and cross-architecture $\sigma$ across five configurations. $p$: exact permutation test (two-tailed, 120 permutations). $\sigma$ values reported $\times 10^{2}$ (accuracy percentage points). Ratio: $\sigma_{\text{pure}} / \sigma_{\text{h3-1}}$. \textbf{Bold}: only benchmark with $\rho = -1.0$.}
\label{tab:convergence}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
Benchmark & Category & $\rho$ & $p$ & $\sigma_{\text{pure}}$ & $\sigma_{\text{h3-1}}$ & Ratio \\
\midrule
\textbf{LAMBADA} & \textbf{Retrieval} & $\mathbf{-1.0}$ & \textbf{.017} & \textbf{2.41} & \textbf{0.49} & \textbf{4.87} \\
BoolQ & Comprehension & $0.2$ & .783 & 1.63 & 2.36 & 0.69 \\
ARC-C & Reasoning & $0.6$ & .350 & 0.96 & 1.32 & 0.72 \\
HellaSwag & Reasoning & $0.3$ & .683 & 1.17 & 1.18 & 0.99 \\
PIQA & Reasoning & $-0.5$ & .450 & 0.54 & 0.34 & 1.59 \\
WinoGrande & Reasoning & $0.1$ & .950 & 0.67 & 0.78 & 0.86 \\
ARC-E & Mixed & $-0.7$ & .233 & 1.00 & 0.13 & 7.70 \\
MMLU & Mixed & $-0.7$ & .233 & 0.92 & 0.41 & 2.27 \\
SciQ & Mixed & $0.1$ & .950 & 0.87 & 0.74 & 1.19 \\
XStoryCloze & Mixed & $-0.6$ & .350 & 0.82 & 0.55 & 1.49 \\
\midrule
COPA$^{\dagger}$ & Reasoning & $-0.67$ & .267 & 2.63 & 0.47 & 5.57 \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize $^{\dagger}$Supplementary ($n = 100$); excluded from multiple comparison correction.} \\
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize Bonferroni-adjusted $p = 6 \times 2/120 = 0.100$ (exact); 0.102 arises from rounding raw $p$ to .017 before multiplying.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\subsection{Scale-Dependent Convergence}\label{ssec:scale_convergence}

We compare the LAMBADA convergence pattern at two scales. At 1.3B parameters, convergence is monotonic: $\sigma$ decreases at every attention level from 0\% through 25\% ($\rho = -1.0$). At 340M, convergence is partial. $\sigma$ reaches a maximum reduction of 56\% at intermediate attention ratios, but rebounds at h3-1, yielding a net endpoint reduction of 36\%: the trajectory is non-monotonic, and the highest-attention model does not achieve the lowest cross-architecture variance. One possible interpretation is that at smaller scale, the capacity overhead of attention layers creates interference that offsets the retrieval benefit.

This contrast is a preliminary observation from two data points (not a scaling law) that scale may facilitate complete equalization. Despite the aggregate divergence, mean pairwise Jaccard similarity is similar at both scales (340M: $J = 0.094$ helped, $0.090$ hurt; 1.3B: $0.100$, $0.093$), extending the decoupling observation (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}) to the scale dimension.

\subsection{Per-Example Divergence}\label{ssec:per_example}

Do architectures converge by improving on the same examples? We compare each architecture's pure-recurrent baseline against its h3-1 hybrid on 5{,}153 LAMBADA examples, classifying each as helped (correct only in hybrid), hurt (correct only in pure), or unchanged.

Each architecture displays a distinct profile of per-example changes (Table~\ref{tab:per_example}). GDN loses a net 153 examples (462 helped, 615 hurt), RetNet loses 69 (426/495), and HGRN2 gains 153 (548/395). HGRN2 is the only architecture for which attention produces a net per-example benefit. Pairwise Jaccard similarity is low: mean $\bar{J} \leq 0.104$ for helped, $\leq 0.096$ for hurt, indicating ${\sim}90$\% non-overlap. Overlap significantly exceeds the independence baseline ($J_{\text{null}} \approx 0.05$, all $z > 5.9$, $p < .0001$; Appendix~\ref{app:jaccard_permutation}), but normalizing by the theoretical upper bound $J_{\max} = \min(|A|,|B|)/\max(|A|,|B|)$, observed overlap reaches only 5--7\% of the achievable range above chance (normalized ES 0.065 helped, 0.062 hurt; per-pair details in Appendix~\ref{app:normalized_es}), confirming that low Jaccard cannot be attributed to set-size constraints.

No surface-level feature (token count, word frequency, syntactic complexity) reliably separates helped from hurt examples. However, pure-model confidence on the target token is strongly predictive within each architecture: hurt examples have lower target-token probability among pure-correct examples ($d = 0.83$--$0.92$), and helped examples have higher probability among pure-wrong examples ($d = 0.73$--$0.77$); see Appendix~\ref{app:confidence_margin}. A Kruskal-Wallis test confirms cross-architecture structure in both hurt ($p = .0005$) and helped ($p = .020$) distributions. The confidence margin thus identifies a per-example predictor of which examples will be affected: attention disrupts marginally correct predictions and rescues near-miss errors, but with architecture-specific thresholds that produce the observed cross-architecture divergence. This structured per-example disagreement despite similar aggregate performance constitutes predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive} arising from architectural variation rather than random initialization \citep{damour2022underspecification}.

\paragraph{Multiplicity spectrum.} Extending the per-example analysis to BoolQ ($N = 3{,}270$) and HellaSwag ($N = 10{,}042$) reveals that predictive multiplicity is not binary but varies systematically across tasks (Table~\ref{tab:multiplicity}). The ambiguity $\alpha$ \citep{marx2020predictive} spans an order of magnitude: from 0.10 (HellaSwag) through 0.21 (ARC-Easy) and 0.30 (LAMBADA) to 0.49 (BoolQ). Yet pairwise Jaccard remains uniformly low ($\bar{J} \leq 0.10$, all $p < .01$), indicating that architectures affect different examples regardless of how many examples are affected. HellaSwag's near-zero $\alpha$ rules out measurement noise as the source of multiplicity on the other benchmarks. Within each benchmark, $\alpha$ is stable across attention ratios (range $\leq 0.04$), confirming that multiplicity magnitude depends on task, not attention dose \citep[cf.][]{gorbett2026crossmodel}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Predictive multiplicity at 1.3B (h3-1). $\alpha$: ambiguity \citep{marx2020predictive}. $\bar{J}$: mean pairwise Jaccard of helped~(h) and hurt~(u) sets. Benchmarks ordered by $\alpha$.}
\label{tab:multiplicity}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrcccc@{}}
\toprule
 & $N$ & $\alpha$ & $\bar{J}_{h}$ & $\bar{J}_{u}$ & $\rho_\sigma$ \\
\midrule
HellaSwag & 10042 & .10 & .091 & .080 & $-0.8$ \\
ARC-Easy & 2376 & .21 & .092 & .096 & $-0.2$ \\
LAMBADA & 5153 & .30 & .086 & .091 & $-1.0^*$ \\
BoolQ & 3270 & .49 & .103 & .098 & $+0.1$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\par\vspace{1pt}
{\footnotesize $^*\!p = .017$. $\alpha$ stable across ratios (within-benchmark range $\leq 0.04$).}
\end{table}

\subsection{Decoupling}\label{ssec:decoupling}

The central finding of this paper is that aggregate convergence and per-example divergence operate on different timescales as attention ratio increases (Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling}).

Cross-architecture $\sigma$ decreases gradually across the five attention ratios: $0.024 \to 0.016 \to 0.014 \to 0.006 \to 0.005$. In contrast, pairwise Jaccard similarity between architectures' per-example effects saturates at the first attention level (4\%): approximately 0.095 for helped, with no further increase at higher ratios. Net asymmetry between architectures' per-example profiles increases from 0.013 at 4\% attention to 0.040 at 25\%.

This separation of timescales constitutes a decoupling: attention reduces the aggregate performance gap but does not homogenize per-example behavior. Bootstrap confidence intervals (10{,}000 resamples) confirm this statistically: the 95\% CI for $\sigma$ does not overlap between pure $[0.019, 0.030]$ and h3-1 $[0.002, 0.011]$, while the CIs for mean Jaccard overlap completely across all non-zero attention ratios (helped: $[0.08, 0.11]$; hurt: $[0.07, 0.11]$). Within-architecture Jaccard averages ${\sim}0.33$ (Appendix~\ref{app:within_arch_stability}), roughly three times the cross-architecture value, confirming that architecture identity has greater influence than attention dose on which examples are affected.

Attention weight analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:attention_patterns}) provides partial insight: GDN and RetNet produce nearly identical attention distributions at layer 2 (cosine 0.995) yet differ in per-example effects, and entropy differences between helped and hurt examples are negligible ($|\Delta H| \leq 0.018$), suggesting per-example effects originate in the recurrent layers. The four-benchmark spectrum (Table~\ref{tab:multiplicity}) confirms independence from aggregate equalization: BoolQ ($\alpha = 0.49$) has the highest multiplicity despite no convergence ($\rho = +0.1$), while HellaSwag ($\alpha = 0.10$, $\rho = -0.8$) trends toward convergence with minimal multiplicity.

\subsection{Robustness}\label{ssec:robustness}

Removing GDN weakens the LAMBADA convergence from $\rho = -1.0$ to $\rho = -0.7$ ($p = .233$), partly confounded with reduced statistical resolution at $n = 2$ architectures. The RetNet--HGRN2 pair preserves the decoupling: all hybrid $\sigma \leq 0.008$ (${\approx}$54\% below pure) while Jaccard saturates at 0.086--0.108, confirming the result is not an artifact of the GDN head-count confound (Appendix~\ref{app:gdn_confound}). Full sensitivity analyses appear in Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity}.

The confound is specific: GDN's h3-1 configuration uses 16 attention heads rather than 32 in all other configurations, introducing an attention-capacity asymmetry. The aggregate convergence result is therefore partially dependent on GDN; the decoupling result is not.

